  "prediction": "**Improper Payments in Federal Programs: Challenges and Recommendations**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) has released a report highlighting the challenges and limitations in estimating improper payments in federal programs. The report focuses on six agencies and 10 programs, including the Department of Defense (DOD), the Office of Personnel Management (OPM), and the Internal Revenue Service (IRS).\n\nThe report identifies several issues with the improper payment estimation process, including:\n\n1. **Inadequate risk assessment**: Some agencies, such as DOD and OPM, have not fully assessed the risks of improper payments in their programs, which may lead to inaccurate estimates.\n2. **Insufficient testing**: Agencies may not be testing for key risks, such as eligibility, which can result in improper payments going undetected.\n3. **Inconsistent treatment of nonresponse cases**: Agencies have different approaches to handling nonresponse cases, which can lead to inconsistent estimates.\n4. **Understatement of improper payments**: Agencies may be understating improper payments by excluding recovered overpayments from their estimates.\n\nThe report makes several recommendations to address these issues, including:\n\n1. **OMB guidance on testing procedures**: The Office of Management and Budget (OMB) should develop guidance on how agencies test to identify improper payments, including a risk-based approach.\n2. **OMB guidance on nonresponse cases**: OMB should clarify the treatment of nonresponse cases during improper payment testing.\n3. **DOD and OPM process assessments**: DOD and OPM should assess their processes for estimating Military Pay and Retirement improper payments to determine whether they effectively address key risks.\n4. **IRS methodology update**: The IRS should update its improper payment estimation methodology to include all improper payments, regardless of whether they have been or are being recovered.\n\nThe report emphasizes the importance of accurate improper payment estimates in understanding the extent of the problem and developing effective corrective actions. The GAO recommends that agencies and OMB work together to address these challenges and improve the accuracy of improper payment estimates.",
